      "hints": [
        "Differentiate: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2(x+1) - (x+1)^{-2} = 0 \\implies 2(x+1)^3 = 1$.",
        "Second derivative: $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2 + 2(x+1)^{-3}$.",
        "Substitute $(x+1)^{-3} = 2 \\implies \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2 + 2(2) = 6$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Differentiate $y = (x + 1)^2 + (x + 1)^{-1}$ and set to zero for the stationary point $A$:\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2(x + 1) - (x + 1)^{-2} = 0$$\n$$2(x + 1) = \\frac{1}{(x + 1)^2} \\implies 2(x + 1)^3 = 1$$",
        "Find the second derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$:\n$$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2 - (-2)(x + 1)^{-3} = 2 + 2(x + 1)^{-3}$$",
        "Substitute $(x + 1)^{-3} = 2$ from $2(x + 1)^3 = 1$ to evaluate $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ at $A$:\n$$\\left.\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\right|_A = 2 + 2(2) = 2 + 4 = 6$$"
      ],
